1
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:08,190
Oh, I always tear up

2
00:00:08,250 --> 00:00:10,420
When the Grinch's heart grows three sizes.

3
00:00:10,490 --> 00:00:12,450
Tears seem appropriate.

4
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:14,120
Enlargement of the heart muscle,

5
00:00:14,190 --> 00:00:16,490
Or hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, is a serious disease

6
00:00:16,550 --> 00:00:18,550
which could lead to congestive heart failure.

7
00:00:22,850 --> 00:00:25,090
You really didn't like it, Sheldon?

8
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:26,590
No, on the contrary.

9
00:00:26,650 --> 00:00:31,090
I found the Grinch to be a relatable,engaging character.

10
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:33,750
And I was really with him right up to the point

11
00:00:33,820 --> 00:00:35,450
that he succumbed to social convention

12
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:38,450
And returned the presents and saved Christmas.

13
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:40,590
What a buzz kill that was.

14
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:45,450
When we watch Frosty the Snowman, he roots for the sun.

15
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:47,090
Excuse me,

16
00:00:47,150 --> 00:00:49,850
But the sun is essential for all life on Earth.

17
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:51,220
Frosty is merely

18
00:00:51,290 --> 00:00:55,620
A bit of frozen supernatural ephemera in a stolen hat.

19
00:00:55,690 --> 00:00:58,720
A crime, by the way, for which he is never brought to account.

20
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:31,430
I just love decorating the Christmas tree.

21
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:33,300
It makes me feel like a little girl again.

22
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:35,530
We didn't have a tree when I was growing up.

23
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:37,060
Really? Why not?

24
00:01:37,130 --> 00:01:38,900
Mmm, in my family,

25
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:41,230
Holidays weren't so much celebrated as studied

26
00:01:41,300 --> 00:01:43,730
For their anthropological and psychological implications

27
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:45,060
On human society.

28
00:01:45,130 --> 00:01:47,060
Oh, sounds festive.

29
00:01:47,130 --> 00:01:48,700
Did you at least give presents?

30
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:50,460
Mmm, in a way.

31
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:52,530
We presented papers.

32
00:01:54,060 --> 00:01:57,230
And then broke off into focus groups and critiqued each other.

33
00:01:58,360 --> 00:01:59,460
Sheldon, what about you?

34
00:01:59,530 --> 00:02:00,900
Did you have a Christmas tree?

35
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:03,230
Oh, yes.

36
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:05,760
We had a tree, we had a manger,

37
00:02:05,830 --> 00:02:07,430
We had an inflatable Santa Claus

38
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:09,760
with plastic reindeer on the front lawn.

39
00:02:09,830 --> 00:02:11,350
And to make things even more jolly,

40
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:13,700
there were so many blinking lights on the house

41
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:16,360
They induced neighborhood-wide seizures.

42
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:19,260
So I take it you don't want to help us trim the tree.

43
00:02:19,330 --> 00:02:20,460
I do not.

44
00:02:20,530 --> 00:02:21,960
But if you insist

45
00:02:22,030 --> 00:02:25,430
on decorating a spider-infested fire hazard in my home

46
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:27,930
I would request that you add this.

47
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:29,260
What is it?

48
00:02:29,330 --> 00:02:31,930
You're kidding, right?

49
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,900
It's a bust of Sir Isaac Newton.

50
00:02:35,930 --> 00:02:37,160
Oh, sure, sure.

51
00:02:37,230 --> 00:02:38,430
Very Christmasy.

52
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:40,360
Wait, excuse me, but it's much

53
00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:42,930
more "Christmasy" than anything you've put on the tree.

54
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:43,930
Here we go.

55
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,430
December 25, 1642,Julian calendar,

56
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:50,030
Sir Isaac Newton is born.

57
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:52,100
Jesus, on the other hand, was actually born in the summer

58
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:54,030
His birthday was moved

59
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:56,030
to coincide with a traditional pagan holiday

60
00:02:56,100 --> 00:02:57,860
that celebrated the winter solstice

61
00:02:57,930 --> 00:02:59,630
with lit fires and slaughtered goats.

62
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:01,260
Which, frankly,

63
00:03:01,330 --> 00:03:03,730
sounds like more fun than 12 hours of church with my mother

64
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:05,230
followed by a fruitcake.

65
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:08,860
Merry Newton-mas, everyone.

66
00:03:08,930 --> 00:03:11,860
I sense that's not sincere, although I have no idea why.

67
00:03:13,130 --> 00:03:14,700
No, it's fine. Look, Sir Issac can go

68
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:16,130
right next to this little candy cane.

69
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:18,960
No. Isaac goes at the top of the tree.

70
00:03:19,030 --> 00:03:20,730
No, he doesn't.

71
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:22,160
I understand.

72
00:03:22,230 --> 00:03:24,860
You dispute Newton's claim that he invented calculus

73
00:03:24,930 --> 00:03:27,900
and you want to put Gottfried Leibniz on the top.

74
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:29,100
Yeah, you got me.

75
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:30,430
I'm a Leibniz man.

76
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:32,260
Well, perhaps when your mother gets here,

77
00:03:32,330 --> 00:03:33,800
she'll talk some sense into you.

78
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:35,000
What?

79
00:03:35,060 --> 00:03:36,600
Your-your mother's coming? When?

80
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:37,600
Tomorrow.

81
00:03:37,660 --> 00:03:39,360
When were you going to tell me?

82
00:03:39,430 --> 00:03:41,530
Um... tomorrow?

83
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:44,130
Why were you keeping this a secret?

84
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:45,360
Well, I just-- I thought...

85
00:03:45,430 --> 00:03:47,200
If I can interject here,

86
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:49,030
Obviously Leonard is concerned

87
00:03:49,100 --> 00:03:51,430
that his mother won't approve of you as his mate.

88
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:54,260
Why wouldn't she approve of me?

89
00:03:54,330 --> 00:03:55,900
I'm adorable.

90
00:03:55,960 --> 00:03:57,730
You are, it's just...

91
00:03:57,800 --> 00:04:00,230
If I can interject again.

92
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:03,900
Leonard comes from a remarkably high-achieving family,

93
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:05,930
who have all chosen high-achieving partners.

94
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,560
He probably feels

95
00:04:07,630 --> 00:04:10,360
that it's doubtful that his mother will be overly impressed

96
00:04:10,430 --> 00:04:13,200
with his dating a woman whose most significant achievement

97
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:16,130
is memorizing the Cheesecake Factory menu.

98
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:18,330
Hey, it's a big menu.

99
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:21,500
There's two pages just for desserts.

100
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:22,730
I know.

101
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:24,930
And those specials-- they change every day.

102
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,400
Okay, you know what? It's lame when I say it.

103
00:04:27,460 --> 00:04:29,430
- It's just ridiculous when you pile on. - Okay.

104
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:30,760
Sorry.

105
00:04:30,830 --> 00:04:32,760
So what did she say when you told her we were going out?

106
00:04:32,830 --> 00:04:34,960
Um...

107
00:04:35,030 --> 00:04:37,330
You didn't tell her we were going out, did you?

108
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:39,460
Um...

109
00:04:39,530 --> 00:04:40,960
Why not?

110
00:04:41,030 --> 00:04:43,300
- Um... - Leonard...

111
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:44,900
I'm no expert on meditation,

112
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:46,530
but if you're trying to calm yourself down,

113
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:48,300
I believe the word is "Om."

114
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:53,060
It was so nice of you

115
00:04:53,130 --> 00:04:55,100
to come all the way down to the airport to pick me up.

116
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:56,460
No trouble at all.

117
00:04:56,530 --> 00:04:58,530
I drove, Mother.

118
00:04:59,730 --> 00:05:01,660
I'm driving now.

119
00:05:01,730 --> 00:05:03,200
Yes, dear. Mommy's proud.

120
00:05:04,360 --> 00:05:06,130
I've been meaning to thank you

121
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:07,360
for your notes on my paper

122
00:05:07,430 --> 00:05:08,930
disproving quantum brain dynamic theory.

123
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:10,130
My pleasure.

124
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:11,700
For a non-physicist,

125
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:13,360
you have a remarkable grasp

126
00:05:13,430 --> 00:05:16,030
of how electric dipoles in the brain's water molecules

127
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:18,830
could not possibly form a Bose condensate.

128
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:20,300
Wait, wait, wait.

129
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:23,200
When did you send my mom notes on a paper?

130
00:05:23,260 --> 00:05:25,060
August 16.

131
00:05:25,130 --> 00:05:27,260
Right after her carpal tunnel surgery.

132
00:05:28,730 --> 00:05:30,330
- Oh, did I thank you for the flowers? - You did.

133
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:32,030
I don't really like flowers.

134
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:34,000
Neither do I, but it's the social convention.

135
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:36,160
It is, isn't it?

136
00:05:36,230 --> 00:05:38,160
Wait, wait, wait. You had surgery?

137
00:05:38,230 --> 00:05:40,000
Yes, and Sheldon sent me flowers.

138
00:05:40,060 --> 00:05:41,330
No, I heard that.

139
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:44,000
Then what was all that "wait, wait, wait" about?

140
00:05:44,060 --> 00:05:45,660
I just don't understand

141
00:05:45,730 --> 00:05:48,500
why he knows more about your life than I do.

142
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:50,130
Well, I would assume it's because Sheldon

143
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:52,400
and I stay in touch due to mutual interest and respect,

144
00:05:52,460 --> 00:05:53,760
while you avoid me,

145
00:05:53,830 --> 00:05:56,230
due to unresolved childhood issues.

146
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:57,560
It's what we think

147
00:05:57,630 --> 00:05:59,960
caused your narcissistic personality disorder.

148
00:06:01,430 --> 00:06:04,360
We discussed it at length during our last video chat.

149
00:06:04,430 --> 00:06:07,560
Although how we got onto the subject of you is baffling.

150
00:06:07,630 --> 00:06:09,900
Yes, but we are on the subject,

151
00:06:09,960 --> 00:06:11,600
so I'm obliged to ask.

152
00:06:11,660 --> 00:06:13,930
Leonard, how are you?

153
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,360
Fine, Mom. How are you?

154
00:06:16,430 --> 00:06:17,730
Mmm, menopausal.

155
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:21,360
Now I'm less fine.

156
00:06:21,430 --> 00:06:23,260
Have you heard your brother has gotten engaged?

157
00:06:23,330 --> 00:06:24,760
No.

158
00:06:24,830 --> 00:06:26,760
Sheldon, why didn't you tell me?

159
00:06:26,830 --> 00:06:28,600
My bad.

160
00:06:28,660 --> 00:06:31,200
I did send a gift from both of us.

161
00:06:31,260 --> 00:06:33,000
She's a remarkable girl.

162
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:35,000
The youngest appeals court judge in New Jersey

163
00:06:35,060 --> 00:06:37,530
and a two-time Olympic bronze medalist.

164
00:06:37,630 --> 00:06:39,560
You must be very happy.

165
00:06:39,630 --> 00:06:41,360
Why? I'm not marrying her.

166
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:44,160
So, how about you?

167
00:06:44,230 --> 00:06:45,900
Are you seeing anyone interesting?

168
00:06:45,960 --> 00:06:48,060
Well, I'm not sure about interesting, but...

169
00:06:48,130 --> 00:06:49,860
Not the time, Sheldon.

170
00:06:49,930 --> 00:06:51,060
Very well.

171
00:06:51,130 --> 00:06:52,760
Shall we switch topics

172
00:06:52,830 --> 00:06:55,800
to Isaac Newton v. Gottfried Leibniz?

173
00:06:55,860 --> 00:06:57,630
It's all right, Sheldon.

174
00:06:57,700 --> 00:06:58,830
I will just pretend

175
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:00,730
that Leonard's not withholding information.

176
00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:03,400
Although, I will point out, Leonard,

177
00:07:03,460 --> 00:07:05,430
that I am a trained psychiatrist

178
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:08,260
and you are exhibiting the same secretive behavioral tics

179
00:07:08,330 --> 00:07:10,530
that accompanied your learning to masturbate.

180
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:16,500
Isn't she brilliant, Leonard?

181
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:19,200
How I envy you.

182
00:07:20,560 --> 00:07:22,000
So, Howard,

183
00:07:22,060 --> 00:07:23,830
have you and Rajesh finally summoned the courage

184
00:07:23,900 --> 00:07:28,130
to express your latent homosexual feelings toward one another?

185
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:33,530
What? No.

186
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:34,960
Why not?

187
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:38,960
Because we don't have latent homosexual feelings

188
00:07:39,030 --> 00:07:40,360
toward one another.

189
00:07:40,430 --> 00:07:42,360
I see.

190
00:07:42,430 --> 00:07:44,360
No, really.

191
00:07:44,430 --> 00:07:46,030
I have a girlfriend now.

192
00:07:46,100 --> 00:07:47,460
And where is she this evening?

193
00:07:47,530 --> 00:07:49,130
She had to go out of town.

194
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:50,630
Her grandmother died.

195
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:52,730
I see.

196
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:55,160
Her grandmother died.

197
00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:58,230
Honest to God.

198
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:00,400
Leonard, tell her I have a girlfriend.

199
00:08:00,460 --> 00:08:02,460
I don't know what you're talking about.

200
00:08:02,530 --> 00:08:05,300
What do you mean you don't know what I'm talking about?

201
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:06,560
Tell her I have a girlfriend!

202
00:08:06,630 --> 00:08:08,730
All right.

203
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:10,730
He "has" a "girlfriend."

204
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:14,330
Her name is Bernadette. She's working as a waitress,

205
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:16,700
but she's going to school to be a microbiologist.

206
00:08:16,760 --> 00:08:18,560
Howard, keep in mind that the more passionately

207
00:08:18,630 --> 00:08:19,730
you stick to this construct,

208
00:08:19,800 --> 00:08:21,130
the more you're hurting your partner.

209
00:08:25,460 --> 00:08:28,460
Do you really think your lips in my ear is helping?

210
00:08:29,500 --> 00:08:31,760
Hi. Sorry I'm late.

211
00:08:31,830 --> 00:08:33,200
Oh, glad you're here. Uh, sit down.

212
00:08:33,260 --> 00:08:34,230
I'll get you a plate.

213
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:35,560
Mom, you remember Penny.

214
00:08:35,630 --> 00:08:37,430
Oh, yes, the waitress slash actress

215
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:40,660
with the unresolved father issues.

216
00:08:40,730 --> 00:08:41,930
Has he finally come to terms

217
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,930
with his little slugger growing breasts?

218
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,960
Well, he sent me a football and a catcher's mitt for Christmas,

219
00:08:48,030 --> 00:08:50,300
So I'm going to say no.

220
00:08:50,360 --> 00:08:53,360
If it helps, we're all good with your breasts.

221
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:58,400
Classic overcompensation.

222
00:08:59,760 --> 00:09:01,400
Oh, speaking of fathers, Leonard,

223
00:09:01,460 --> 00:09:03,430
That reminds me, I'm divorcing your father.

224
00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:05,630
What?!

225
00:09:05,700 --> 00:09:07,030
Yes. He was cheating on me.

226
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:09,030
- No!  - Yes, with some waitress

227
00:09:09,100 --> 00:09:10,660
From the university cafeteria.

228
00:09:10,730 --> 00:09:11,860
Can you believe it?

229
00:09:11,930 --> 00:09:13,900
A waitress?

230
00:09:13,960 --> 00:09:15,960
Oh, no offense, dear.

231
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,430
No, it sounded like a compliment.

232
00:09:20,830 --> 00:09:22,000
When did this happen?

233
00:09:22,060 --> 00:09:23,300
Mmm, well, let's see.

234
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:24,960
Sheldon, when did I leave Leonard's father?

235
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:27,300
September 22.

236
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:28,460
Oh, yes, that's right.

237
00:09:28,530 --> 00:09:29,800
The weekend after Leonard's dog died.

238
00:09:29,860 --> 00:09:31,860
Mitzy's dead?!

239
00:09:33,460 --> 00:09:35,400
She was old and blind, Leonard.

240
00:09:35,460 --> 00:09:38,230
What choice did we have?

241
00:09:38,300 --> 00:09:39,360
I don't believe this.

242
00:09:39,430 --> 00:09:41,000
Why am I the last to know?!

243
00:09:41,060 --> 00:09:43,160
Excuse me, Leonard, I am the one who's getting a divorce,

244
00:09:43,230 --> 00:09:44,560
Mitzy is the one who is dead.

245
00:09:44,630 --> 00:09:46,800
Why are you the one making a fuss?

246
00:09:46,860 --> 00:09:48,060
You're right.

247
00:09:48,130 --> 00:09:49,100
I'm-I'm-I'm-I'm sorry.

248
00:09:49,160 --> 00:09:51,160
I'm way out of line!

249
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,430
So, Penny, what's new in your life?

250
00:09:56,500 --> 00:09:57,460
Nothing.

251
00:09:57,530 --> 00:10:00,200
Not a damn thing.

252
00:10:06,100 --> 00:10:08,840
Thank you for driving me back to my hotel.

253
00:10:08,900 --> 00:10:10,340
Oh, it's not a problem.

254
00:10:10,400 --> 00:10:12,400
I was going to ask Leonard to do it,

255
00:10:12,470 --> 00:10:14,700
But he seemed a bit emotionally unstable

256
00:10:14,770 --> 00:10:18,000
And you don't want someone like that operating heavy machinery.

257
00:10:18,070 --> 00:10:19,940
No, you do not.

258
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,400
Your "check engine" light is on.

259
00:10:22,470 --> 00:10:26,240
Yeah, I gotta put a sticker over that.

260
00:10:29,040 --> 00:10:32,000
So, uh, you must be devastated about your divorce.

261
00:10:32,070 --> 00:10:33,340
Oh, not at all.

262
00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:36,370
But I am a bit distressed to be in a vehicle

263
00:10:36,440 --> 00:10:39,200
That's not subjected to regular maintenance.

264
00:10:39,270 --> 00:10:41,440
Come on, I mean, you're not upset

265
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:42,870
that your marriage is over?

266
00:10:42,940 --> 00:10:45,220
Well, initially I felt something akin to grief

267
00:10:45,220 --> 00:10:47,500
And perhaps anger,

268
00:10:47,570 --> 00:10:50,600
But that's the natural reaction of the limbic system

269
00:10:50,670 --> 00:10:53,500
To being betrayed by a loathsome son of a bitch.

270
00:10:53,570 --> 00:10:55,540
Sure, sure.

271
00:10:55,600 --> 00:10:57,770
Thankfully, my shock was somewhat mitigated by the fact

272
00:10:57,840 --> 00:11:00,300
That I haven't had intercourse with him in eight years.

273
00:11:00,370 --> 00:11:02,140
